<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liverpool and Manchester Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Manchester Railway, opened in September 1830, was the first inter-city railway in the world to rely entirely on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive">steam locomotives</a> and to carry both passengers and freight on a timetable. Built with George Stephenson as engineer and known for the Rainhill Trials won by the Stephensons' Rocket, it set the pattern for the railway age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway.jpg" alt="Liverpool and Manchester Railway" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.B. Clayton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opening_Liverpool_and_Manchester_Railway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> raw material came in through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> for the textile mills near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a>, and much of the finished cloth went back for export. The canals and the Mersey and Irwell Navigation were felt to be making excessive profits and throttling the growth of Manchester, and the turnpike road was poor. The canal operators and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a> and the Earl of Sefton, over whose land the line would pass, opposed a railway.</p><p>The company was founded on 20 May 1824, with Henry Booth as secretary and treasurer, and George Stephenson was appointed principal engineer that June. The first bill was thrown out on 31 May 1825 after Stephenson, under cross-examination, could not account for his levels or costs. A second bill passed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Manchester Railway Act 1826.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The board reappointed Stephenson as engineer, with Joseph Locke as his assistant. The line was 31 miles long. It began with the Wapping Tunnel under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, the first tunnel bored beneath a metropolis, and ran through a deep rock cutting at Olive Mount and over a nine-arch viaduct across the Sankey Brook valley. Contracts to drain Chat Moss were let in June 1826.</p><p>At the opening on 15 September 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a>, the Prime Minister, rode in the special train, which stopped for water at Parkside. William Huskisson, Member of Parliament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, got down to speak to him and was struck by the locomotive Rocket, which shattered his leg. He died at Eccles, the first widely reported railway passenger fatality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The railway cut the journey between the two towns to two hours, and most stage coach companies soon closed. It ran excursion trains within weeks and paid investors an average dividend of 9.5 per cent a year over the 15 years of its independent existence. Goods carrying began only in December, once the more powerful locomotive Planet arrived. The company's concentration on passengers had effects across the country and helped set off the railway mania of the 1840s.</p><p>It was the first railway to be double track throughout, to have a true signalling system, to be fully timetabled and to carry mail. Policemen posted a mile or less apart signalled to the trains, which were kept apart by intervals of time. The gauge later widely adopted came from a Stephenson recommendation accepted by the board in July 1826. The Grand Junction Railway absorbed the line in 1845.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liverpool and Manchester Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cotton textiles in Lancashire, coal, iron and engineering, and from 1830 the railways, beginning with the <b>Liverpool and Manchester Railway</b>, made Britain the ‘workshop of the world’, as displayed at the Great Exhibition of 1851.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
